<Section id="force" title="2. A Force-Directed Cloud">

The packing was tidy and still. This time we let the discs **move**. A **force simulation** gives each disc a little physics: they drift, bump into one another, and settle — and we nudge them so that discs of the same **genre** drift together into loose clumps, with each genre pushing the others away. No fixed grid, no anchors; the layout just emerges.

It's also **interactive**. The circle under your cursor shoves nearby discs aside, so you can carve a path through the cloud, and the **slider** changes how many songs are in play at once — drag it and watch the simulation re-settle.

<Bleed max={1200}>
	<ForceGraphDemo />
</Bleed>

{#snippet sidebar()}

A force layout is fiddly to hand-tune but easy to _describe_ — a good candidate for drafting with an AI assistant, then adjusting the strengths by feel. Try this prompt:

```text
/// file: Prompt to try
I have a dataset of 2010s songs (title, artist, genre, popularity, bpm,
energy, danceability).

Build an interactive D3 force-directed chart in Svelte. Each song is a
small "vinyl disc" sized by popularity. Cluster the discs by genre using
a same-genre attraction plus charge repulsion (no fixed anchors), and add
collision so they don't overlap.

Keep a circle under the mouse that repels nearby discs as it moves. Add a
slider that controls how many songs are shown at once, starting at 50.
```

> [!Tip] Things to try
> - **Drag the slider** to add or remove songs and watch it re-settle.
> - **Sweep the cursor** through a cluster to push the discs aside.
> - Same-coloured discs drift together — those are the genres finding each other.

{/snippet}

</Section>
